      "prompt": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "solution": "First, let $u=x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
